From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 04:40:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] drm/i915/dp: Dolby Vision tunnel through a DisplayPort to
 HDMI converter

A DisplayPort connector of a port that can carry the tunnel through the
converter of its board (intel_dv_pcon_policy.h) gets DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB
and DVBRIDGE_REFUSED as an HDMI connector does, and DVBRIDGE_DV_CONVERTER,
read only: what detect found behind the port, "none", "unlisted"
(a converter that nobody has seen carry it), "listed" (the board is on
the list) or "forced" (dv_pcon=1). Kodi's Automatic takes "listed" and
"forced", its setting "On" takes "unlisted" too; "none" is refused by the
kernel whatever user space asks, as before. Low-Latency and the HDMI 1.4b
signalling are not attached to a DisplayPort connector: they were not
seen through a converter.

The converter is "behind the port" when the sink is an HDMI sink behind a
DisplayPort branch device of the HDMI type that will not go to FRL
(what the tunnel was seen through is TMDS). The kernel log says it when
detect finds one, with the verdict and why (the board on the list, or
not; dv_pcon), and warns once when a request goes through one that
nobody has seen carry it.

The configuration is checked with intel_dv_lab_validate() as the one of
native HDMI is, with the converter test of the policy in place of the
native HDMI one, and DVBRIDGE_REFUSED says why a request is refused
(RGB, 8 bits, full range, no HDR metadata, no DSC, a mode in the list,
a sink that takes it). The tunnel never goes past the converter's TMDS
limit (no retry without the downstream limits), as through native HDMI.

The AVI InfoFrame of the tunnel (drm_dvbridge_tunnel_avi(), as native
HDMI sends it) and the Dolby vendor InfoFrame go to the converter as
DisplayPort InfoFrame SDPs, as HDR10 does: the AVI one in the AVI DIP,
the vendor one in the GMP DIP (the vendor DIP blanks the HDMI output of
the converter that this was seen through, YBLOD's report); the converter
turns them into HDMI InfoFrames. The DP InfoFrame SDP has room for 26
payload bytes: the last byte of the Dolby vendor InfoFrame is reserved
and zero, and is checked to be. The GMP DIP carries the vendor
InfoFrame in the guardband calculation of the SDPs too.

Not tried on hardware. Compiled with W=1, for i915 and xe, with the
configuration and the toolchain of the image.
